---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFileBase64 from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeFileBase64.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="file-to-base64">
  <DevEncodeFileBase64 client:load mode="file" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why anything gets Base64-encoded</h2>
    <p>
      Many systems can carry only text. JSON has no binary type. Email bodies are text. YAML manifests,
      environment variables, database <code>TEXT</code> columns, XML payloads and log lines are all text.
      Base64 is the standard way to send arbitrary bytes through those channels, so that nothing in the
      middle reads a byte as a delimiter or a control character.
    </p>

    <h2>What people actually encode here</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>File</th><th>Where the Base64 goes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>TLS certificate or private key</td><td>A Kubernetes <code>Secret</code>, which requires Base64 for every value</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Service-account JSON</td><td>A CI environment variable, so a multi-line file survives as one string</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PDF invoice</td><td>A JSON API field on a billing or e-signature endpoint</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WOFF2 font subset</td><td>An inline <code>@font-face</code> in a single-file HTML report</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Small ZIP or firmware blob</td><td>An embedded-device payload sent over a text protocol</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Short audio clip</td><td>An <code>&lt;audio src&gt;</code> data URI in a self-contained page</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Kubernetes secrets, specifically</h2>
    <p>
      Each value under <code>data:</code> in a Secret manifest must be Base64. People often make two
      mistakes here. First, <code>echo "value" | base64</code> also encodes the trailing newline. Second,
      Base64 in a Secret is encoding, not encryption: anyone with read access to the object can decode it
      immediately. Use <code>stringData:</code> to let the API server do the encoding, and use a real secret
      manager for confidentiality.
    </p>

    <h2>Size, and when to stop</h2>
    <p>
      The file to Base64 encoder works in 3&nbsp;MB slices with a progress bar, so the only hard limit is the
      memory of your device. The practical limits are in the destination:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Most JSON APIs reject bodies over a few megabytes.</li>
      <li>Kubernetes limits a Secret to 1&nbsp;MiB of encoded data.</li>
      <li>Browsers historically limited <code>data:</code> URIs in the address bar to about 2&nbsp;MB.</li>
      <li>Editors and diff tools become unusable well before any of these limits.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The 33% expansion applies before all of these limits, so a 1&nbsp;MB file is already 1.37&nbsp;MB of
      text.
    </p>

    <h2>The trailing-newline trap</h2>
    <p>
      Almost all &ldquo;my Base64 does not match the documentation&rdquo; reports come from one byte.
      <code>echo</code> adds a newline; <code>printf</code> and <code>echo -n</code> do not. Encoding a file,
      as this page does, never adds one. If the value here differs from your shell output by four characters
      at the end, this is the cause.
    </p>

    <h2>Encoding a private key on a web page</h2>
    <p>
      This is a fair question. This page runs in your browser, and the Content-Security-Policy of the site
      lists no third-party origin in <code>connect-src</code>, so the browser would block an upload even if
      the code tried one. Disconnect from the network, and the tool still works. This is a technical
      guarantee, not a privacy policy. In 2025 an online PDF service left about 3.5 million user documents
      publicly reachable.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
